## Why This Is Asked
Tests designing a practical, on-device ML flow that balances accuracy, size, privacy, and deployment realities.

## Key Concepts
- On-device constraints: memory, CPU, quantization
- Feature engineering: cyclical time, geospatial bucketing, hashed categories
- Model choice: compact linear models with proper regularization
- Privacy-aware validation: leave-one-user-out or per-session splits
- Evaluation: AUROC, calibration, appropriate thresholds

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny logistic regression dot-product (illustrative)
function predict(w, x) {
  let s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < w.length; i++) s += w[i] * x[i];
  return 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-s));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle missing values on-device?
- How would you validate robustness to distribution shifts after deployment?

## Why This Is Asked

Production ML systems must adapt to concept drift and data skew without sacrificing stability. This question probes concrete, actionable strategies beyond theory.

## Key Concepts

- Concept drift detection
- Sliding window retraining
- Score calibration and monitoring
- Safe deployment and rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal drift check sketch (conceptual)
function detectDrift(scores, windowSize, threshold) {
  const w = scores.slice(-windowSize);
  const mean = w.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)/w.length;
  return Math.abs(mean - 0.5) > threshold;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the rollback path before production deployment?
- Which telemetry would you collect to prove drift handling works?

